        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>Scarcely ar hour mimeo without iMportant thaw., being made in the arrangements relating to Commemora- tion week, and proofs under revision" are flying about •• thickly and mesa results to date" during • general : election. Her Majesty is constantly impressing upon all parties concerned, the necroirity of simplifying every. thing Sc much.. porsible, of taking the most elaborate and anxious precautions against fire and accident, and, further, of bearing in mind the rights of the people. As • precaution against Are, on Jubilee Day, it him been arranged to eetabliM engine. and fire escape. at earl°. need pointa, and • correepondent to one of the London papers has suggested that the County Council Should let out the latter as .• unequalled sates' for viewing the prn- CE0.013.</p>
        <p>—The-fetter which the Queen has moiled to adders. to I her eubjects in acknowledgment of the loyal congratu- lation. on the f scasion of the celebration of the sixtieth year of her accession to the 'Throne, will be forthcoming &amp;Dant theend of the month. It will of course, appear In the first imtance In the newspapers, but owing to the repressed desire of the Sovereign amnion,' nte have been made for publishing copies of the &amp;Lib graph letter , in facsimile, and et • price that will make its p,e•ession quite pomible to all hut the abeclutely destitute. One practical good thing the Jubilee hOOM hae done to Greater Britain in, it ha• advertised the "alt red route " by which the Australasian§ and toe (7anadiane I and their kinsfolk ill the roost EaJtern regions of the Far East mean, the Pacific route through Canada to the Old Country. The Pacific is, as we all know, the world's property, but the land part of the route is wholly through the British realm. Undoubtedly one of the most adventurois women of our time is Miss Mary Kingsley, • niece (and not • daughters. was recently stated by some of the re... view...) of Charles Kingsley. Alone thin intrepid Afri- can eriplorer has ventured tkrungh countries regarded as among the most dangerous both on account of cli- mate and of the character of the inhabitants. Just swith of the Niger Company'. territories info be found the Canierrapas, • country nominally belonging to tier. many. !farther .00f:still is the French Congo. Through those countries wink • climate reputed deadly to the white man, and with inhabitants who are among the most ravage in Africa, Miss Ktng.l.s has made her firer. She fmn travelled on foot • hundred through • country, parte of which are infested by can. nibals,travereed rivero in a hat with • crew of negro, and ascended moutitaine 10.000 feet high, wick only her little body guard of the same racv. Mr. P. Wealberley, an Engliell traveller, lately en. pined Lake lisegwooto, null then •inited Claitambo, where the heart of David Livingstone line buried beaide the Chief Chitamisf who gave a name to the spot. No. thing marks the place except a decaying inpundu tree,, which the Devitt. r describes es mere crumbling Own Livingstone died near the apot, hot it will moon toe lost sight of Mr. Weatherley says,unfees marked by • memo-. Sal oh • permanent charaCter. Whit. Monday was the day fixed upon for the le•Ptism of the little daughter of the Duke and Ducher of York, lat Sandringham. The infant Princess wore the Royal christening robe, and wan handed to the Archhiahnp hn her youthlublooking grandinother,the Princesa.d Wales, whose Mth grandchild she Is, and received the name of Victoria Ale x.dra Alice Mary. The christenia7 cake , measured about 38 inch... in height, and 20 ini hen in I diameter, and weighed 70Ihe.</p>
        <p>In graOces, christening in much more of an event than with us, in fact, it makes almost as much stir in a household as a wedding. When the day has been decided upon and the gedparenta cho es, the mother of the babe invitee .11 the relatives on both !WON of the house and many sif the friends to ;twist et Ile cere•mony at the church, and to return afterwards to the Ont.e to drink to the health rf no•lrdiaptised. To the Rnalletfier ,• falls the olatigation of giving a present to the mother of :the babe and of course to the all-important infant, and it is his duty to provide boars of sweets to be presented by the godmother, ono to each guest on the great °cm- Mon.</p>
        <p>-.Vine godmother's privilege is to clothe the little one for the esremony Irani top to to, Matting with thie re- sponsibility it is not surprising that the godmother plays such an important part in the life of most French girls. Every step is referred to her judgment, such es the choice of governess, masters or convent school, sod later on the retuning or accepting of the proposal of • euitor. Indeed • French godmother is • veritable guar- dian sogel, whn osly colloidet her duties discharged when tire godchild in married: •: •</p>
        <p>The (71-ty'lorporation h. insued a notice to women who will occupy Corporation sea, on the 221 d ins% begging them •'. an set of grare " to •• kindly appear nn thin occasion in bonnet. or hats with limited trim. wawa'. The notice applies I ormelly only to Corporation Neat., but it would be a very ....Rent thing 11.11 yr. men who prepose .llyinl forth with the idea of WY, log the proce•aion.would hear the hint in mind. At the present moment, judging by the whiners' showroom., there, little hope that 1/offP mod. trimmed hat. will he entirely ...rifled in the public interest for small toques , temneta on the all-important it...</p>
        <p>And epesking of the garden. in II ,Iver-pots and strew hunt,, that crown the feminine head just now, I an, re- minded of the pastor of a Chicago chapel,whn ha. taken a the,logical view of our headgear, and condemned it.. reprmenting the vanity hf the rrld. From long experi• pm, he has coins to the conclusion that it ie quite imp.- tilde for us to concentrate our minds on our devptione If we keep our hats on to church, while he has notidd how • Invariably the entrance of a line specimen distract, the feminine attention and encourages envy, malice and all uncharitakleneas. Thu gond past,r determined there- fore to call the attention of his women svorabippen to the evil thing, and after laming a little tract on the queCion appealed to there to take off their hats on enter. mg the chapel the neat Sunday. The reeult of his re- queet is pot yet bemire. A delightful novelty has lately appeared in the Maw of an admirable kicking bid glove, warranted to etand any amoinet nt hard woe. and moreover to wash with eimple soma and water on the hand without injuring the leather at all. It is a clever and capital invention, and entirely and perfectly practitial which half them intro- ductions certainly are not. 'Meese. en few article, that satisfactorily receive their freehnese, hut this is one of them. . . .</p>
