        <p>IN the horror of those moments e Pugh felt nerveless, and after the first sr o attempt to shriek there was no necessity . chasol pressed so tightly over her lips (0 .11. WI /yid by a strong arm and carried beck a few 40 , and thee held firmly against the bulwarlour The next moment, as/frith starting eyes she and wildly about in earch of help, her captor'. lips were placed l, her, and wools that ...mod to scorch her heal -ere h,ssadinto her ear. Have I not w ed you sufficiently? Bot, for the intense love I year you, this moment would be your last. One nge, it would be impossible to nave you in th,s. rkness, and no oneewould realise who did the d d. Do you wish me to make use of the knowleil I gained tovlay with those dyna- rnite experini its ; because, listen, I have not looked on in an, One touch of a wire -one that I have laid and thin ship and all on hoard woukl be in frag nets. That would have happened if you hail no forward to-night and heti-eyed or. Once in re, listen ; it in useless for you to light againat. your fate, for I ain not alone here; and when limas° watching you others take up the task. There: See, I release and trust you after what I have !said."</p>
        <p>He: took his hand from the trembling woman's lips, 'hut grasped her tightly still, lost she Rhould sink down fainting. Now return quietly to your cabin," he con- tinued, and remember this. You think to save Dutch Pugh and the rest by betraying me. Instead of that you will send the, to their I leath. Now go.tmek without a sound." ' '</p>
        <p>Hester felt her aria relexseil, and that she was free. Her first will thought wan (circa forward, shrieking for hel her next that Isturt. would keep his word, and, controlling hernelf, she tottered with ontstretche hands back to the cabin stairs, anti reached the little cabin where liessywan already asleep, and then, sinking on her knees, proved for help in this time of 110.1. e • • • • •</p>
