        <p>'The was on the point of going tin deck 'Ali, Lanni's words atayed her. tithe wan watched, ouni if slic tried to communicate with her husband might he not interpret it as 00 attrutpt to betray loin, and in an instant compsem tin destruction. "If I only knew what to do !" she moaned. "If I timid but warn him of the langer they might wine that villain in time. I will warn him at all hazards."</p>
        <p>She was ready to die to save Dutch front peril, hut she was circumstanced that her warning would compass his destruction, and she sank hiwk feeling at last that she could not betray what die knew. For the rnotnent she wan reassured by hearing Dutch's voice, and directly after Itensy Came to fetch her into the cabin to breakfast, where all save she were in high spirits, no one having a suspicion of the danger that threatened then,. The talk Man al; of the treasure, and the specimen ingots that Laure had shown then, were nientioned, while to Ilester's horror she found that the Cuban Wan apparently forgotten. It aim] lsecame evident to her that all the pre- parations had hoen made, and she followed the actors in the busy scene hi be enacted on deck 1101 soon as the hurried meal was at an end.</p>
        <p>Dutch had glaneml at her once, and her heart throbbed with pleasure as she read hi look as one more id sorrow than anger, and this lagt determined her to simak to him at all hazard,. The pr pomp was ready, with Hasp dictating and ordermg the men about and had Heater felt any hesitation before, the sight of Duteh draw ing ~n the heavy imlia-rubber suit determined her to et.</p>
