        <p>;;i7;," not afraid of that, Harp," said Dutch, Laughing ; and then, aa he stood with the helmet en hi, arm, he turnol cold and .tore again, tor on env, Hotter approach, end.. she did so the other. involuntarily drew awsy. " What in it?" he cord, coldly. 4' Dutch," .he whinpered, no nhe laid her hand, upon his shoulder., "your true, faithful nib, who hae never wronged you in thought or deed, implore. you to take the precaution they auk." "PM " he exclaimed, contenirtuously. " You 110 not believe me, dear, ' .he continued, with the tears atreaming down her cheeks ; "but (;011 ix my judge that I speak the troth. Oh, Dutch, Dutch !" she continued, as RIO' now his fare begin to work, "erne day you will know all, and your heart will bleed for the agony you have caused me." " Hester," he aaid in the same low tone," I'd give twenty years of ivy life to have hack the same old trust in you, but it in gone, gone for "No," she replied, with a bright look homing in her face, "it to not the truth in twining—com- ing noon, and when it does, Dutch, you will come back to my heart with the knowledge that your little wife has forgiven you your injuntive front the ant, that she Noe you MOT dearly than ever." " You forgive etc?' he amid bitterly "Ye,, the wrong you have done vie, Dutch. You have nothing to forgive me but for keeping eiy neeret for your cake." There was such an air of candour and truth in her countenance that had they been alone he would have clutchol her to bin brew., but he knew that they were watched liv ninny eyee, and restraining himself he Font oinetly "it is enough now. Tell me thin—will you— when I return--</p>
