        <p>"Not he, imam, while old Toni Rasp is alive to help," gnmled the old fellow. " Perhaps you'd better go below, my dear," said Mr. Parkley. said Hester firmly, and drawing herself op " I shall stay." "Then von shall, my dear hut." he added, with a mule, "woman for ever ! You've won the day her going to have the tile vole'." The old doubts, a ri, lu had 6,1 growing fainter and which would, MO doubt, have been entirely swept away by an explanatnal, came hock more strongly again at Hester's refusal, and with a feeling of rage and 14terness hitch raised the helmet, placed it upon his head, and signed to old Rasp to mine and screw it on. This the old fellow did after securing the extra life.line to his belt, hut not before Ihitxdt had had a few words with Mr. Parkley act,, the ~,,,,,,tement of the ilynainite and wire., . . . .</p>
        <p>The men on the look-out could see no itharke, all being apparently gluite clear, and at last, when with hatchet and knife in his belt, and the wheel of the air-pump beginning hi clank, Dutch moved towardn the gangway, trailing after him the long India-rubber tube, tiere wan a loud cheer, and everyone leaned forward in eager excitement. " Now to mlve the problem. Stuilwick," mid Mr. Parkley, who was evidently excited, and who dabbed hin face bigot rid of the dripping perspira- tion. "Initto be luck or ill-luck I' "That I'll tell you bye-and-bye," mid the imptain, smiling ; and like Mr. Wileon and the doctor, he stood up on the bulwark. to help to keep • cowl look-out for sharks. " Now, look here, Mr. Parkley," mid limp, who bad assumed the management, and dictated as it every thing belonged to him, "just you place Mr. Jones the mate here with three men to let that there lifeline run softly throtmh their heade when it'e pulled, and to heave in tin. Mack when it not;i but when I give the word they're to run it in with all their might—take hold of it, you know, and run along the deck." Heeler l'ugh's breath caught, as now, with dilating eyes, she watched her husband, who, as calmly as mosible, stepped on to the ladder, and began to desoend step by step, till his shoulders were immersed, when he paused fer • moment to alter the way in which the tube hung from his helmet; then Rasp, paring it through his hand., and giving • word or two of advme to the men at the pump, the helmet disappeared beneath the surfam, and in place of the hisstng noise heard as the air escaped from the valve, there come foaming up a continuous stream of bubbles through the !Impel water. The men gave another cheer, and the Cuban, who had crept round dose to Hester, looked down over the bulwark, full of muitmity to me what would follow.</p>
