        <p>Iting4 —et lovewet..i IF, M- -4400 CO,4IOLnkFLII LS Ur, aeld• 11110OL</p>
        <p>didarencm and to combine together nta solid * force in politica—Probably therefore Mr. Man- waifs exhortation to gd is for politloi will, no for as Scottish co-operators #ro concerti., fell upon willing oars, though in England the cry of " No politics in co-operation '' lien not,we believe, lost any of its old power. With regard to th general program of co-operation the reports to tho present Congribui do not wand to show any falling off. There was, it is true, a note of alarm raised with regard to the ohm increase in the number of societ im, but this was accounted forty the growth of *eine of the larger sociotim whose rapidly developing branches app.j to cover ground that would otherwieff be occupied by several smaller societies. Tho statistics submitted oh tit, that, whether the number of separate societies intreasee or decrea.s, the total membership of the Co. operative army was augmented last year by thou- sands of new recruits while the sales were enlarged ' to the extent of four or tivo inillions of pounds. With respect to the several Friendly Societies whooo delegates have met in conference this week there also appmirs to he something like an all- round nrord of progress. The meeting at Douglas of the NI anchester Vnity placed the I :rand Mas- ter in a poeition to make a striking minparison. It ltaa so long ago mg Intl When the Ord, last hold its conference in the popular Manx watering place, and the interval of tifty-aix years covers a fairly long period of the growth id oven an old- aatablishod friendly society. The differ.cs be- ' twoen now and thou g.l truly xiginfinalit. to 1011,</p>
        <p>the Nitinchetter Unity coo,isted of 171fii 11 morn- hors; iii 1114 of January thin year the roll of members nuoilierod no finer than 769,9n9 —a really wonderful growth occur during a period when there lies boon a goody incro nee in tho goneral population of the country. Ono of the most in- teresting, if ut one of the roost important, ufoon- tine debated at those e,inferuneea this year was the subject of " wet rents, raised at D eights our</p>
        <p>Tuesday. An investigation eieninitteo reported 1 that one lodge pail as muoli as twelve shillings ' per night for the uss of. room, and in return received six gallons of beer to be consumed by the members. The custom was roundly and</p>
        <p>unreservedly condemned ey.the Committee', which</p>
        <p>in fact recommended that the strongest meager. ' should be taken against the trustee§ of the lodges where tint disgraceful and pernicious System " was in vogue. It mainly lair to any that the " wet " system hail ita defenders. St. Helmut Is one of the indicted dietrict• anti • St. Helens delegate declared that " he believed the practice had brought members and strength to the Unity," and he did not think it was prohibited by either " common law " or " ecclesiastical law." But whatever may be the " ecclesiastical law " upon the point it is certain that • majority of the dele- gates wore dead against" wet mote," and it looks as though • decided attempt is to be made to put an unit to there so far as the Menenester Uuity is concerned.</p>
        <p>Nunisu could be more expressive l`Pteritldt of the change which has come over the polit:cal situation than the re- • lief which the TOri. 113rivo Iron the result if the Peterstield election. After Walthamstow ucl Crewe this party, which carried everything before ' it at the General Election,is humbly thankfnl if it esti come through a contest with the loss of only half its majority. Tut ix what it has done at l'etersliehl and in these days it is content to due craw this us a " Tory victory.-lii I'M! when the Liberals secure,' a Parliainentary majority, the Liberal candidate for Petersliold wax defeated by the uhetantial majority of MIL and in 1895 the Liberal proepect wu couidered so hopeless that the Conservative Was allowed a walk our. Yet in the same constituency on Tuesday the adverse majority woe rothicett to 42.0, sod the fact that the Torino; look i.fno, this as a satisfactory re- suit shows how eunciona they are that they have lost the confidence of the cotintry. What- ever tier cpponenta may ha pleaswl to think or. soy, the Liberal party has every :lemon to be utiefied with the result of this eleabni. Peterw field is • teirely rural conatittioncy ni i,clltis a locality in which the peculiar method.</p>
        <p>of the Primrose Limo.. eintlit 1..011 in tho tno•t</p>
        <p>_ effective way. It is, therefore, not an ideal con- stituency in which to light a test battle agnionst the forces of the present Government. Yet the - voting protet that the Goverunseot is losing ground even in the rural districts in the South of England which aie looked upon as thin stronghold of Toryism in this country. The contest appears to have been fought alinonit entirely upon the foonton of foreign policy, and it should not as a reminder to Lord gALIABI'I, that his ilesertion of the Armeunans and the Greeks ..ind his sub- servience to the three autocrats are cruising thin indignstica of Englishmen in all parts of the county.</p>
        <p>The Rector of Warrington quite recently referred in public to the outepokennem of the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, of flurtonwioil. There have been many evidences rd this, especially in the" Burhouw,”d Church and Parish Chronicle' fir which he in res• vonsible. In the current loon., there aro two para- graphs which clearly show that he follows • with keen Interest minter, concerning the town miwhich ; he laboured for • number of yews. "The loyalty of Warrington" he says "I. always on puede. It is • town, I. everybody knows, ureter the special pro- ion.e of two very voreerful bresvors. Their ;bot- tom-el, the their mcnoy, are almost unlimited. Loyalty and beer, however, on Chi ncewaion are ap- parently not on the mine side, for the proposed J ilolee Memorial has ended in ignominious failure. Warrington Joics not atand by itself in being unable to carry through its Jubilee where.; hut other pieces are not no beautifully loyal' as Warrington, awl have not the same opportunity of calling the trade •to their militant, Warrington town yeas • Wanderer ever, soil will maintain its reputation un• • bl•Mishiel, until it places in ofthe citizena who think more of the town and townsmen than of politica and party until it itocepm the Oospol of A Item- iim. The children me to have • feed, and the aged pour silo, m 1.290 worth iff squibs are tr. bring the , great eiimmemoral i tla close, if, that is, the .C9O Can be got together - awl we hope it will not." In this lag hope, the writer is doomed to dimpe poor mm'. ,Th • " have already been ordered, and, we believe. the money for them sub- scribed. - the other paragraph relating to Warrington tom Teter* ate to the profit.soaring achetn• which has rocently been introduced by Mown. Richmond and Compay. It reads so follows : •-•• We note with elacere ple•oure that lie--em. Richmond Co. of *neningron. Inert, made their business • profit- •hari on, 11nm...forth their reeplosSne well re- evens small pereentao of the trodti, in addition to their wageo,end in proportion to the dividends. ; * In milt efiering lies,we enhoilt, the solution of the I.l6uur r.fbirm. it is the Christiaa orlulion Lay. I , generally adopted, would aher in a r..., :tovrt-al ml-trial pia,. 11.1. y o'eptel,l Let re, ry work stop be a do; oi, or Ake of Mel</p>
        <p>Labour Brotherhood,' and so of Ch Socialism Mews. Richmond have chosen the very beat way of commensorating the thaen's Long Reign.-</p>
        <p>Those people who utilWed the week-end for a short holiday have had the hest thet Whitson- I tido afforded thin year. Saturday, Sunday, and Monday were almost wholy dry and warm, so the enjoyment wee given to thousends. Most of the business houses an! shops in Warrington we e c:osed on Monday and freedom thus given to the townspeople who at once betook themselves to varioax in/oared resorts. The throe stations were kept bevy weh pleasure se.kera destined for Ches- ter, Ifelsby,FroilYham, Lyinio, Manchester, Liver- pool, Southport •nil other places, while the reitular buses to Latchford and filLockton Heath carried a large complement. Cyclists were elite to enjoy greater mope, and the districts they visited in such /ergs numbers are too numerous to mention. Co fortunately the holkday did not pays over without castialties,ohY of which hay been fatal to alry.aleeejs of flyrithside who Was thrown from a landau near the Fire Brigade Stetion on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton entered into the Bridge-street property question like e bon, and left it like a lamb. At the May meeting of the Town Council his warmest indignation wan excited by the state- !mint that the improvement in Brlilge-street, waa rielayo I by the extortionate demand.. of property owners, and he after.wardi gave notice ot a question to be asked at the next Council meeting. Thu question was of the ilotible.barrelle I kind. It anted when the Committee intended to proceed to the purchem of propertien In Bridge-etreet ' and e whet wan the reel calm, of the preennt delay:' But when thstelderman was called upon on Tuenilay to pet hie question he, in his very blandest , way. withdrew the none., The C/Orniite. ha I. he nail, dive overeil that the charge a making ex- tortionate demand, was not correct no far as some of the ownern were concerned. Therefore, Alder- man Sutton withdrew, as gracefully is ponsible, from the militant poeition he had previounly taken Thia wa, mutely a larnil and impotent conclu- sion. It loft the matter exactly where it way be- fore. The statement that thin very important etreet improvement was delayed by the extortion-1 ate demands of property owners rouseil Alderman Suttotia ire at the May meeting, yet at the June meeting he gi wee a. e reation for withdrawing !tic quention that only ism, of the property owner, are asking exorbitant prices. So loran the street im prorementieroncernel the distinction is withou. a ihiTernnec, aol the protein, owners Can hall], thank Alderman Sutton for the way in which he hae dealt with Om pubbict.</p>
        <p>The other queotions by Councillors Tinnitn end Fleming with regard to the property now in the band, of the Clip 'ration did not bring out much information. The Corporation ha during the last few years been a large buyer of property for street and other improvements, and tile quantity it has now in hand must be very cunsiderahlii. The ques- tions of Mr. Tninien awl Mr. Fleming were framed with a view to obtaining information an to the total cost of all think, purchase., and the annual profit or loss that they entail- Op fl tho town. Thin information would, ?nit might supproe,tio very nee- ful to members of the Council, and exile., tingly interesting to the ratepayers generally, him it was not given on the groom] that purchanes which are still uncompleted might be I ryjuiliced.</p>
        <p>An interesting little ifiecuftion took place upon the proprowl to pay member, of the Council and elliciala who go to London on Corporation bust new the cum of the. per day and first.claw railway fare. Councillor Tlnnion thought this was. rather too liberal allowance, but he was only supported by one other member --Councillor Fern.. --In his pro- posal to refer the subject back. The members of the Council nvidentlydlonk they are fully entitled to the afis. a Jay. Apart, however, from the amount of the allowance, ante which there is room for dif- ference of opinion, it is no doubt the best policy to ! definitely fit the •nanunt. Ii in at any rate the ! policy which commends itself to the Finance Corn- mitten which has hail the "deputatora. "little billy ! to pane Thew bilk seem to have varied consider- ably in the put, but in the future they will be !regulated by the rewlution adopted on Tuevlay.</p>
        <p>Some talk with omenl to the jubilee programme was inevitable. On the whole, it in satisfactory tn know that there will be no more Council meetings before Jubilee Day. The arrangementn for the 22nd are definitely fixed and now that the time for cad- Oct.= it parsed there .111.M.1 to, be a tienertl desire to make the heat of them. The celebration Di War-1 'lesion will almott entirely take the form of a children's lettiral, and, given tine weather, there is I every proopect that the young people will have an ! enjoyable time. The Corporation will not beatow any medals, Ins, till, the other hand, the Mayor in- I tends no fire every child trio.. threepenny piece, I if the neeese my number of new coins can be got I ..41 of. C.obably if. vote could be taken amongst the childrrn her,, would be a huge oi•jority in favour of the threepenny pieces rather than the medals.</p>
        <p>There has beton nd °Scud 111UMBLi011 SA VI the Ham. of the woo:bonen who it la rumoured are to be placed en the C00P111.10,1 of the peace for the borough. it is generally understood, howevee. that the following Pi, gentians. n base been selected : Mr. Richard T. rairclough, Mr. James Ilepherd, Althorns. Blachlturst,Gounctilor Sham tirorn,Coun- oilier Dr. Canoell. and Councillor Sow burst. In Werenee to this subject ws may quote the !allow- ing paragraph from the London correspondence of the Manchester Guardian " :—" It IA rumoured among the supporters of the Oovernmsnt that the Chancellor ot the Duchy ot Lancaster intends add- ing quit... numb, of flint.s to the 1,0111111i6.11.1 of the peace for Lance.hiro, both c :tint; and borough. Jubilee day will probably he considered a lit occa- sion for announcing the appointments.</p>
